in thrombosis"]}, "B2.3": {"id": "B2.3", "label": "Acute Liver Congestion from Right Heart Failure", "parent": "B2", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.14620377590098305, "posterior": 0.14620377590098305, "danger": 0.75, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.65, "interrupt_relevance": 0.75, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": ["right heart failure"]}}, "frontier": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3", "B2.1", "B2.2", "B2.3"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the subacute onset of pain over days with recent weight loss and exercise-induced worsening favor a chronic hepatic vascular process like Budd-Chiari syndrome?", "summary": "Weight loss and subacute presentation are less typical of chronic hepatic vascular disorders, which usually present with more gradual symptoms."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the recent weight loss and use of oral contraceptive pills increase the likelihood of gallstone-related biliary obstruction?", "summary": "Weight loss and OCPs are risk factors for gallstones, but the acute hemodynamic instability suggests a more severe mechanism than typical biliary obstruction."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of documented amenorrhea or adnexal tenderness in the physical exam weaken the case for gynecologic emergencies as the primary diagnosis?", "summary": "Normal pelvic exam findings reduce the immediate likelihood of gynecologic emergencies, though hemodynamic instability remains concerning."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Are the acute presentation and lack of chronic liver signs (e.g., ascites, JVP) inconsistent with hepatic vascular disorders?", "summary": "Acute onset without chronic liver manifestations makes Budd-Chiari syndrome or hepatic vein thrombosis less likely."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the pattern of hemodynamic instability (80/40 mmHg, 110 bpm) inconsistent with biliary tract obstruction as the primary mechanism?", "summary": "Classic biliary obstruction typically does not cause profound hemodynamic instability, making this explanation less likely."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of blood in stool or urine, and no reported trauma, argue against spontaneous splenic rupture or traumatic hepatic injury as the cause?", "summary": "Lack of traumatic history and hematochezia/hematuria reduces concern for traumatic causes of intra-abdominal hemorrhage."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B2", 0.439], ["B1", 0.407], ["B1.1", 0.163]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": true, "reason": "Hemodynamic instability (hypotension 80/40 mmHg, tachycardia 110 bpm) with high suspicion of ruptured ectopic pregnancy (B1.1) due to reproductive risk factors and shock pattern. Requires immediate stabilization and β-hCG confirmation.", "required_actions": ["Administer IV fluids aggressively", "Monitor for signs of hemorrhagic shock", "Prepare for ultrasound to assess for intra-abdominal bleeding"]}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "[static mode override] Urgent hemodynamic instability and high suspicion for time-critical conditions (e.g., ruptured ectopic pregnancy) require immediate stabilization and diagnostic imaging over further differential expansion. — emergency_override suppressed in static_diagnosis_qa mode"}, "turn_budget_used": 1, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.85, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.16289707143738816, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely the diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Ectopic implantation of a blastocyst"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Inflammation of the pancreas"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Obstruction of blood flow through the hepatic vein"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Obstruction of the common bile duct by radio-opaque stones"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Vascular ectasia within the liver"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Splenic rupture'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B2", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.439}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B1.1", "label": "De Novo Ectopic Pregnancy", "posterior": 0.163, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 0, "disconfirming": 0, "last_disconfirming_digest": ""}, "action_intents": [{"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B2.1", "B2.2", "B2.3"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=75%, source=both]\n\nAcute Intra-abdominal Hemorrhage vs De Novo Ectopic Pregnancy:\n  Favours first:  blood in stool, blood in urine, bruising, clammy skin, cold skin\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n\nAcute Intra-abdominal Hemorrhage vs Ruptured Ovarian Cyst (e.g. Corpus Luteum Cyst) with Hemorrhage:\n  Favours first:  blood in stool, blood in urine, bruising, clammy skin, cold skin\n  Favours second: abdominal bloating, abnormal vaginal bleeding, feeling of fullness in the abdomen, fever, increased urinary frequency\n\nAcute Intra-abdominal Hemorrhage vs Budd-Chiari Syndrome with Acute Presentation:\n  Favours first:  blood in stool, blood in urine, bruising, clammy skin, cold skin\n  Favours second: acute hepatic failure, ascites, autosomal recessive inheritance, budd-chiari syndrome, cholecystitis\n\nAcute Intra-abdominal Hemorrhage vs Hepatic Vein Thrombosis from Hypercoagulable Disorder:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, blood in stool, blood in urine, bruising, clammy skin\n  Favours second: abducens palsy, abnormal auditory evoked potentials, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal circulating copper concentration, abnormal cranial nerve morphology\n\nAcute Intra-abdominal Hemorrhage vs Acute Liver Congestion from Right Heart Failure:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, blood in stool, blood in urine, bruising, clammy skin\n  Favours second: abdominal distension, cold and sweaty skin, fatigue, loss of appetite, rapid or irregular heartbeat\n\nDe Novo Ectopic Pregnancy vs Ruptured Ovarian Cyst (e.g. Corpus Luteum Cyst) with Hemorrhage:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n  Favours second: abdominal bloating, abnormal vaginal bleeding, dizziness, feeling of fullness in the abdomen, fever\n\nDe Novo Ectopic Pregnancy vs Budd-Chiari Syndrome with Acute Presentation:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n  Favours second: acute hepatic failure, ascites, budd-chiari syndrome, cholecystitis, cirrhosis\n\nDe Novo Ectopic Pregnancy vs Hepatic Vein Thrombosis from Hypercoagulable Disorder:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abdominal pain, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity\n  Favours second: abducens palsy, abnormal auditory evoked potentials, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal circulating copper concentration, abnormal cranial nerve morphology\n\nDe Novo Ectopic Pregnancy vs Acute Liver Congestion from Right Heart Failure:\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abdominal pain, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity\n  Favours second: abdominal distension, cold and sweaty skin, confusion, fatigue, loss of appetite\n\nRuptured Ovarian Cyst (e.g. Corpus Luteum Cyst) with Hemorrhage vs Budd-Chiari Syndrome with Acute Presentation:\n  Favours first:  abdominal bloating, abnormal vaginal bleeding, dizziness, feeling of fullness in the abdomen, increased urinary frequency\n  Favours second: acute hepatic failure, ascites, autosomal recessive inheritance, budd-chiari syndrome, cholecystitis\n\nRuptured Ovarian Cyst (e.g. Corpus Luteum Cyst) with Hemorrhage vs Hepatic Vein Thrombosis from Hypercoagulable Disorder:\n  Favours first:  abdominal bloating, abdominal pain, abnormal vaginal bleeding, dizziness, feeling of fullness in the abdomen\n  Favours second: abducens palsy, abnormal auditory evoked potentials, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal circulating copper concentration, abnormal cranial nerve morphology\n\nRuptured Ovarian Cyst (e.g. Corpus Luteum Cyst) with Hemorrhage vs Acute Liver Congestion from Right Heart Failure:\n  Favours first:  abdominal bloating, abdominal pain, abnormal vaginal bleeding, dizziness, feeling of fullness in the abdomen\n  Favours second: abdominal distension, cold and sweaty skin, confusion, fatigue, loss of appetite\n\nBudd-Chiari Syndrome with Acute Presentation vs Hepatic Vein Thrombosis from Hypercoagulable Disorder:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, acute hepatic failure, ascites, budd-chiari syndrome, cholecystitis\n  Favours second: abducens palsy, abnormal auditory evoked potentials, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal circulating copper concentration, abnormal cranial nerve morphology"}
